course: dictionary
lesson: 15106
title: Rushing: fast, forceful, or hurried
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word rushing comes from rush. When you are rushing, you move or do something very fast, often because there is not much time. «Lily was rushing to catch the bus.» The bus leaves soon, so Lily must hurry.
explain: Rushing can also describe water or wind. «The rushing river was too strong to cross.» Water that rushes moves fast and with force, so the river is dangerous. The words around rushing tell you which meaning fits.
explain: Rushing can also be a warning. «Don't rush, check your work.» The clue is check: when you rush, you can make careless mistakes. Ask yourself what word would fit, then try your meaning in the sentence to see if it makes sense.
fact: Rushing means moving or doing something very fast, often because there is little time.
fact: Rushing water or wind moves fast and with force.
fact: Rushing someone means making them hurry, as in «The coach kept rushing the team onto the bus.»
fact: «Rushed off one's feet» means very busy.
fact: Rushing on stairs or crossing the road without looking can cause accidents, so take your time.
exercise: «The children were rushing to school because they were late.» What does rushing mean? | *Moving very fast because there is little time | Sleeping in a warm bed | Singing loudly with friends | Walking slowly and looking at the sky
exercise: «The rushing river was too strong to cross.» What is rushing describing? | A quiet pond in the park | A toy car that is stuck | *Water moving fast and with force | A bird flying high in the sky
exercise: Mum says, «Don't rush, check your work.» What is she worried about? | Being too early for school | Losing her keys | *Careless mistakes | Forgetting to eat her lunch
test: «Ben was rushing down the stairs and nearly fell.» Why is rushing on stairs dangerous? | Because stairs are always wet | *Because you might trip and fall | Because you can see too far | Because stairs go up forever
test: Dad says, «There's no need to rush, we have plenty of time.» What does he mean? | Time has stopped | *We do not have to hurry | Hurry up right now | We must run for the bus
exam: In «The teacher kept rushing us to finish the quiz», what does rushing us mean? | Taking us outside for fresh air | Teaching us slowly | *Making us hurry | Giving us a long rest
